-- BLOCKED — do not apply. Renamed to .PENDING so it cannot run by accident.
--
-- WHAT THIS WAS MEANT TO DO
--
-- Happenings-Brief.md §9 ("Repeat additions") asks for:
--
--     ALTER TABLE public.user_option_entries DROP CONSTRAINT user_option_entries_pkey;
--     ALTER TABLE public.user_option_entries ADD COLUMN id uuid NOT NULL DEFAULT gen_random_uuid();
--     ALTER TABLE public.user_option_entries ADD PRIMARY KEY (id);
--     CREATE INDEX user_option_entries_user_day_option_idx
--         ON public.user_option_entries (user_id, day_key, option_id);
--
-- so that the same happening logged twice in one day produces two rows.
--
-- WHY IT CANNOT SHIP AS WRITTEN
--
-- `PRIMARY KEY (user_id, day_key, option_id)` is the ONLY unique constraint on
-- this table (verified against production 2026-08-08). Every deployed client
-- upserts against it:
--
--     SupabaseSyncService+Entries.swift:27
--     on_conflict = "user_id,day_key,option_id"
--
-- PostgREST turns that into `ON CONFLICT (user_id, day_key, option_id)`, and
-- Postgres requires a unique index for ON CONFLICT to resolve. Demoting the
-- constraint to a plain index makes every one of those upserts fail with
-- 42P10 ("no unique or exclusion constraint matching the ON CONFLICT
-- specification") — for all clients in the field, not just old ones.
--
-- Making the replacement index UNIQUE instead would keep old clients working
-- but forbid the duplicate rows this whole change exists to allow. The two
-- requirements are mutually exclusive on one table.
--
-- THE FIX: expand / contract, via a new table
--
-- 1. Add `user_happening_additions` (surrogate `id uuid` PK, no uniqueness on
--    user_id/day_key/option_id). New clients write here.
-- 2. Leave `user_option_entries` and its composite PK exactly as they are.
--    Old clients keep upserting into it, unbroken.
-- 3. Once telemetry shows no old clients writing, drop `user_option_entries`
--    along with the five relaxed category columns, in one later change.
--
-- This matches the rollout principle the brief already states for the category
-- columns — nothing is dropped while old versions are still in the field. The
-- brief simply did not notice that demoting the PK is a breaking change in
-- non-breaking clothing.
--
-- Needs a decision before it becomes a real migration.
